# What the handle SET is told BEFORE one C call: the checks. Cut out of
# `emit/handle_traffic.hero` on 2026-09-25 at the `layout` suite's ceiling,
# along the seam the line itself draws: this module speaks before C runs and
# only reads the set, `handle_traffic` speaks after and is what mutates it.
#
# **`consumes` GOES BEFORE THE CALL, and `acquires` cannot** — defect 071,
# 2026-09-20. A consumed handle's value exists before the call; an acquired
# result does not exist until the call returns, and an acquiring `@`
# out-parameter's cell is not written until then either. So the two ends sit on
# opposite sides of the line, and the ordering is a fact about when each value
# exists rather than a preference.
#
# WHAT IT COST TO LEARN. Until today both ends were emitted after the call, on
# the ground that *the counter rides the call it belongs to … so the two are one
# instruction to read* — a readability reason, and CLAUDE.md § Precedence puts
# robustness at rank 3 and leaves that at none. Measured: a second `consumes` of
# the same local reached C first and the process died inside the real
# deallocator at exit 133 with **no message at all**, while the runtime's own
# stray report — which names this exact program in three sentences — waited for
# an exit the program never reached.
#
# AND THE CASE THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT SAID SO IN ITS OWN HEADER.
# `tests/golden/run/abort-handle-given-back-twice.h`: *"`slot_close`
# deliberately does nothing, so the second release below does not corrupt
# anything on this machine and the case can assert the COUNT rather than a
# crash. Against a real allocator the same program is a double free."* True,
# written down, and never followed by the case that runs it against one. That
# case is `fixedbugs-a-real-deallocator-given-the-same-handle-twice`.
#
# **EVERY CROSSING OF A HANDLE INTO C IS ASKED FIRST** (panel 177's item 1;
# defects 077 and 088). Whatever the parameter's mark, none, `consumes`,
# `transfers`, `retains` or `borrows`, a handle the argument reaches is asked
# `hero_handle_alive` before anything else: is it the dead value a consuming
# call left in its place, or an address whose life ended and that C has not
# handed out since. Before the ends, so a dead handle at a consuming position
# is named as dead rather than as a stray. An `@` cell is asked too, because
# C reads it (the compiler-engineer's condition 2), EXCEPT a cell a handle
# comes OUT through: `reads_cell` below says which, and why.
# The string handed with the check names the crossing, because the line a
# runtime abort can name is the C's and the reader's is the `.hero` file's.
use ast
use source
use handles
use check/state
use check/reaches
use emit/writer
use emit/handle_text
use parse

function before_call(@w: writer.Writer, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64, arguments: [str])
    match decls[decl].kind
        .function_decl fd =>
            name = source.span_text(s, decls[decl].name)
            at: i64 @ 0
            by = consumer(s, fd.value, name: name)

            for p in fd.value.params
                if at < arguments.len()
                    written = state.written_type(c, p.ty)

                    if !written.is_err() && reads_cell(p)
                        crossing(@w, c, s, decls, ty: written.must(), p: p, argument: arguments[at], callee: name)
                at @ at + 1

            at @ 0

            for p in fd.value.params
                if at < arguments.len()
                    written = state.written_type(c, p.ty)

                    if !written.is_err()
                        # The CHECK is before the call and the end is after it
                        # (`handle_traffic.after_call`), so a double release is
                        # still stopped before C runs and a call that succeeds
                        # only sometimes ends the life only when it did. The
                        # runtime counts each check as an end ANNOUNCED, which
                        # is what stops one call taking one handle at two
                        # consuming positions before C (defect 084).
                        if p.consumes
                            announce(
                                @w,
                                c,
                                s,
                                decls,
                                ty: written.must(),
                                value: handle_text.cell_of(arguments[at]),
                                at: at,
                                entry: "hero_handle_ending",
                                tail: ", " + by
                            )

                        # A transfer names where the life goes, and the handle's
                        # own set must name it too (panel 176's item 2): checked
                        # here, before C, like a release.
                        if !p.transfers.is_err()
                            announce(
                                @w,
                                c,
                                s,
                                decls,
                                ty: written.must(),
                                value: handle_text.cell_of(arguments[at]),
                                at: at,
                                entry: "hero_handle_transferring",
                                tail: ", " + handle_text.set_c(s, p.transfers.must())
                            )
                at @ at + 1
        .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => return

# One announcement per handle the argument reaches, each keeping the number of
# the life it is announced for in a local of the call's block, which the end
# after the call hands back (defect 098, `handle_text.life_c`). Locals are
# hoisted to the prologue everywhere else in the emitted C; these live in the
# call's block because they belong to one call, as `hero_when` does.
function announce(@w: writer.Writer, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, value: str, at: i64, entry: str, tail: str)
    suffixes = reaches.handle_suffixes(c, s, decls, ty: ty)

    if suffixes.len() == 0
        writer.line(@w, text: "    /* heroes: a mark on a type that reaches no handle */")
        return
    k: i64 @ 0

    for suffix in suffixes
        writer.line(
            @w,
            text: "    uint64_t " + handle_text.life_c(at: at, k: k) + " = " + entry + "(" + value + suffix + tail + ");"
        )
        k @ k + 1

# Does C READ what this argument holds, so a dead handle in it must be
# refused before the call? A value always. An `@` cell unless its mark says a
# handle comes OUT through it: `acquires` (a life begins in the cell),
# `borrows` (C lends one into it) and `retains` (C hands a reference out into
# it, which `handle_traffic.after_call` tells the set with
# `hero_handle_retained` on the cell after the call). A cell the program
# reuses for such a hand-back may still hold what the last life left, and C
# writing over that is not a read of it: refusing it refused a correct
# program (the landing's skeptic, 2026-09-25, probe `a2e`, `ob_get_shared(out:
# @cert)` after `cert` was given back). A C function that READS a handle and
# adds a reference is spelled `retains` on a parameter without `@`, where it
# is asked; one bound wrongly with `@` reads the dead page and faults on it,
# since the page is mapped with no access, so the wrong binding is loud.
function reads_cell(p: ast.Param) -> bool
    return !p.mutable || p.acquires.is_err() && !p.borrows && p.retains.is_err()

# One `hero_handle_alive` per handle the argument reaches, naming the
# crossing: the argument or the cell, its parameter, the call, and the
# suffix where the handle sits inside a record or a fixed array.
function crossing(@w: writer.Writer, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, p: ast.Param, argument: str, callee: str)
    what: str @ "the argument `"
    value: str @ argument

    if p.mutable
        what @ "the cell `@"
        value @ handle_text.cell_of(argument)
    where = what + source.span_text(s, p.name) + "` of `" + callee + "`"

    for suffix in reaches.handle_suffixes(c, s, decls, ty: ty)
        inside: str @ ""

        if suffix != ""
            inside @ ", at `" + suffix + "`"
        writer.line(@w, text: "    hero_handle_alive(" + value + suffix + ", \"" + where + inside + "\");")

# Who is giving the handle back, and what its own mark would let it pay. A
# call that also ACQUIRES is a transfer of the old kind — `realloc`, `freopen` —
# ending one life and beginning the next owed to its own releasers, so it may
# take back a handle whose set shares a name with its own: `freopen` of an
# `fopen` stream, never of a `popen` one. Panel 176's `transfers` is the
# spelling that names this on purpose; this is what the runtime is told for
# the older spelling.
function consumer(s: source.Source, fd: ast.FunctionDecl, name: str) -> str
    if !fd.acquires_result.is_err()
        return handle_text.quoted(name) + ", " + handle_text.quoted(handles.releasers_c(source.span_text(s, fd.acquires_result.must())))

    for p in fd.params
        if !p.acquires.is_err()
            return handle_text.quoted(name) + ", " + handle_text.quoted(handles.releasers_c(source.span_text(s, p.acquires.must())))

    return handle_text.quoted(name) + ", NULL"

## Tests

# The rule the skeptic's `a2e` changed: an `@` cell a handle comes out through
# is not asked, `retains` included, and every value and every other cell is.
test "a cell a handle comes out through is not read, and everything else is"
    text = "extern \"m.h\"\n    record Ob tag ob\n    function ob_put(a: Ob consumes)\n    function ob_up(a: Ob retains ob_put) -> i32 when 1\n    function ob_out(@a: Ob retains ob_put) -> i32 when 1\n    function ob_new(@a: Ob acquires ob_put)\n    function ob_lend(@a: Ob borrows)\n    function ob_touch(@a: Ob)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    read: [bool] @ []

    for decl in parsed.decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd                                          => read @ read.push(
                reads_cell(fd.value.params[0])
            )
            .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

    assert source.span_text(s, parsed.decls[1].name) == "ob_put"
    assert read == [true, true, false, false, false, true]
